BDD: SDD

Purpose:
  Behavior-Driven Development — define business behavior in executable
  specifications before implementation starts. BDD is the bridge between
  business requirements and technical implementation.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Business behavior definition
  Acceptance criteria
  Scenario design
  BDD test structure
  BDD execution

DoesNotOwn:
  Test implementation code (stays in project test directories)
  Unit/integration/e2e test definitions (bax unit.sdd, integration.sdd, e2e.sdd)

Principles:

  BDD is not testing, it is specification:
    BDD describes WHAT the system should do, not HOW it works internally.
    Unit tests verify HOW; BDD verifies WHAT.

  BDD comes before code:
    No implementation starts before BDD scenarios are defined and accepted.
    This is the business mindset: behavior first, code second.

  BDD is shared understanding:
    Business, QA, and development MUST agree on BDD scenarios.
    Scenarios are written in business language, not technical jargon.

  BDD is executable:
    Scenarios are not documentation — they are executable tests.
    They MUST run and pass before the task can advance.

Format:
  Standard Given/When/Then:

    Given <precondition>
    When <action>
    Then <expected outcome>

  AI compact form:

    BDD:
      balance>=refund
      refund(req)=once

  Human form:

    Given customer has sufficient balance
    When customer requests refund
    Then refund is created once

Structure:
  Every BDD feature MUST contain:
    Feature: <business capability>
    Scenario: <specific behavior>
    Given: <preconditions>
    When: <actions>
    Then: <expected outcomes>
    And: <additional conditions>

Examples:

  Feature: Refund Idempotency

  Scenario: Duplicate refund request
    Given a refund request exists for transaction TX-123
    When the same refund request is submitted again
    Then no second refund is created
    And the original refund result is returned

  Scenario: Concurrent refund requests
    Given a refund request exists for transaction TX-123
    When two identical refund requests arrive simultaneously
    Then only one refund is created
    And both requests receive the same refund result

Ownership:
  Business: defines scenarios and acceptance criteria
  QA: writes executable BDD tests
  Development: implements to make BDD pass

Gate:
  BDD gate MUST pass before implementation starts:
    All scenarios defined
    All scenarios accepted by business
    Executable BDD framework in place

Navigation:
  Project: @project/project.sdd
  Workflow: @workflow/testing.sdd
  TestLevels: @levels.sdd
  TestTypes: @types.sdd
  TestGates: @gates.sdd
  Unit: @unit.sdd
  Integration: @integration.sdd
  API: @api.sdd
  E2E: @e2e.sdd
  ProjectTesting: @projects/example_project/testing/INDEX.sdd

State: +
  Note:
  BDD is a first-class citizen in the .sdd system. Every task with
  business behavior MUST have BDD scenarios before implementation.
  BDD scenarios are stored in the project's BDD framework (e.g.,
  Behave, Cucumber, SpecFlow) — not in .sdd/. The .sdd system
  references BDD artifacts, it does not store them.
